# L5R 5E - SHADOWLANDS: GEOGRAPHY AND LOCATIONS (LORE)

**Version:** 0.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game, 5th Edition - Shadowlands (Chapter 1)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## THE SHADOWLANDS

The Shadowlands are a blighted, corrupted wasteland south of the Carpenter Wall. Created when the Dark Kami Fu Leng fell from the Celestial Heavens and tore open a passage to Jigoku, the Realm of Evil, these accursed lands have spread their corruption outward ever since. The geography is fluid and mutable — locations seem to shift, distances change without reason, and the landscape itself conspires against travelers. The sky is never blue, instead alternating between grim charcoal and burning red. Darkness and cold rain are constant companions.

The Shadowlands Taint corrupts all who enter without the protection of jade. Unprotected exposure carries the risk of corruption, and from that point on, the subject walks on the precipice of shadow. Even jade's protection is temporary, slowly expended as it absorbs the pervasive corruption.

---

### The Nature of Corruption

The Taint warps all five elements. Air becomes a whispering carrier of maddening voices. Earth hardens and cracks, rejecting life. Fire burns with unnatural colors and refuses to warm. Water thickens and grows foul, sometimes appearing as blood. Even the Void is not immune — shugenja report a sense of wrongness in the space between elements, as if something alien peers back through the emptiness.

Corrupted terrain takes many forms: blighted soil that births twisted plants, pools of ichor, forests of bone-white trees, and landscapes that seem to breathe. The deeper into the Shadowlands one travels, the more intense and bizarre these manifestations become.

---

## FALLEN CHRYSANTHEMUM LAKE

Deep in the Shadowlands lies a lake of surpassing, terrible beauty. Its waters are a deep crimson, reflecting the twisted trees that line its shores — trees perpetually in bloom with blood-red chrysanthemum flowers. Petals drift constantly across the lake's surface, and the air is thick with an intoxicating, cloying fragrance. The beauty is deadly: the lake radiates an intensely concentrated form of the Shadowlands Taint.

Those who are exposed to the lake's corruption suffer distinctive mutations — skin turning hard and inflexible, eyes dripping crimson tears, and bony protrusions from the skull and joints. If anyone so Tainted decides to end their life, their body is said to melt into a pool of blood that makes its way back to the lake to spend eternity in the crimson waters.

---

### The Dark Moto

The Dark Moto date from shortly after the Unicorn Clan's return in the ninth century. Moto Tsume, daimyo of the Moto family, declared he and his greatest warriors would ride forth to end the threat of the Shadowlands forever. His monumental hubris destroyed them all. None survived as the humans they once were.

Tsume became enchanted with Fallen Chrysanthemum Lake and constructed Thundering Tide Keep along a bank. He and his Dark Moto reside there, retaining their Ujik culture and fighting discipline while being fully corrupted by the Taint. They train endlessly, displaying a fighting cohesion most Shadowlands beings lack. They remain loyal to their daimyo and believe themselves to be the True Moto. Their immortal leader has conducted a campaign of terror against the Empire for centuries.

---

## FALSE LANTERN GROVE

An apparent oasis of spiritual purity, the False Lantern Grove masquerades as a shrine kept safe by the kami during the night, when the inviting glow of its paper lanterns draws in the foolish and desperate. Under the blessed light of Lady Sun, its truth is revealed: the lanterns are hanging skulls of those who have fallen victim to the oni dwelling within.

The grove is a shrine to Fu Leng and Jigoku, fashioned by a devoted lesser oni who fancies itself a pious shrine keeper. It treats all who approach as honored guests — until they refuse to surrender their jade and submit to the Shadowlands Taint. At night, the oni adopts the guise of an unarmed Rokugani monk. By day, the trees are shown to be withered and dead, and the skulls are plainly visible.

The oni, known as the skull-cleaving oni, believes in Fu Leng's inherent authority and divinity. It prefers to proselytize its faith and win converts to the service of Fu Leng. Those who have fallen to its offers maintain a twisted parody of their old religion, returning to the grove to receive the oni's blessing.

---

## THE FESTERING PIT OF FU LENG

At the dark heart of the Shadowlands lies the Festering Pit — the ancient site where the fall of the Dark Kami tore a hole in the mortal world and opened a doorway to Jigoku. It is the original and ultimate source of the Shadowlands Taint. The sky above the Pit is alternately red with fire or black with smoke, and blood falls from the clouds instead of rain.

The Pit yawns hundreds of yards wide, a gaping maw lined with jagged rocks and shards of obsidian, boring down through the belly of the world. Its lower reaches roil with an inchoate sea of bile and filth from which constantly crawl newly formed monstrosities. Were someone able to descend into this froth, it is believed they would arrive in Jigoku itself.

Near the Pit, the power of the Taint intensifies vastly. Even jade's protection falters, its efficacy measured in hours rather than days. Oni wander freely, and hordes of Lost samurai throng about the Pit's lip. The Crab rarely send scouts near the Pit and do not expect them to return.

### Pit-Forged Obsidian

Among the most terrible manifestations near the Pit is Pit-forged obsidian — an unusually strong material, notable for the potency of the Taint within it, burning to the touch with the fires of Jigoku. Some of the Lost and even some oni forge weapons from it.

---

## THE FORGOTTEN TOMB OF FU LENG

Deep within the Shadowlands lies an utterly inhuman building. Its architecture is alien, with impossible angles no mortal can fully comprehend. Its purpose is infuriatingly unclear. Some legends hold that the Black Scrolls contain the sundered body of Fu Leng. What the Tomb actually contains is a haunting question with no comforting answers.

The area around the tomb is devoid of life. Each step taken toward it leaves an imprint that fills with water, and in certain light, these puddles offer glimpses of a different landscape — gnarled branches of dead trees, cliffs of blood-red stone, or a black starless sky. Gaki, or hungry ghosts, infest the air from twilight to sunrise.

Wicked priests of Fu Leng use the tomb as a temple, performing depraved rituals and sacrifices. They build altars outside and leave grisly offerings made of bones, rusted metal, and obsidian shards. The Obsidian Flower, an ancient entity who was once human, dwells within, whispering to Fu Leng as she slowly shifts pieces across the vast board of time, maneuvering humans toward the end of the Empire.

---

## THE LOST LIBRARY

From a distance, the Lost Library appears as a large spike on the horizon. Up close, it is a tower of black stone that rises so straight and tall that its spiked roof disappears into the sky. No one knows who gathered this profane collection, but for maho-tsukai it is a treasure trove. Lining the inside walls are curved shelves stuffed with crumbling tomes and ancient scrolls, some in languages no Rokugani scholar could identify.

The tower has no visible entrances, only windows high up the walls. Tunnels below lead to a hidden entrance, but it is easy to get lost in this dark, cramped maze. Text sometimes appears scrawled on walls or floors, leaked from nearby books, causing unsettling emotions in those who attempt to read them.

Some books within have achieved something akin to sentience. They leap from shelves, adhere painfully to hands, or whisper promises of knowledge and power. Fallen scholars wander the endless stacks, drawn by the knowledge the kansen offered, their obsession with the library's secrets consuming them entirely.

---

## DAYLIGHT CASTLE (SHIRO HIRUMA)

For over four hundred years, Daylight Castle has been a sinister beacon shining from the Shadowlands — a constant reminder to the Crab of their greatest failure. Once the heart of the Hiruma family's defenses and a linchpin of the First Wall, the castle fell to the Shadowlands in 716 when the oni lord known as the Maw led an army of unprecedented size and power.

### A Glorious Early History

Daylight Castle was established by Hiruma as the seat of his family's power. For centuries it served as the core of Crab defensive efforts. In 314, it was saved from a Shadowlands army by the timely intervention of nezumi allies. In 375, the castle withstood a siege by Usu no Oni while the main Shadowlands force pushed deeper into the Empire.

### The Fall

In 716, the Maw's forces overwhelmed the First Wall fortress by fortress. The defenders of Daylight Castle prepared for a siege but were undone when demonic allies corrupted and reanimated their fallen comrades. Each defender who fell rose again as a new attacker. Despite brief recaptures in 747 and 819, the castle has remained in the Shadowlands.

### A Place of Horror

Four centuries of immersion in corrupted energies have permeated Daylight Castle with terrifying evil. The castle seems to possess a malign will of its own, with rooms and corridors appearing and vanishing or changing shape. It presents each visitor with intimate, individual manifestations of horror.

Notable denizens include the Siren (a shapeshifting creature that assumes harmless forms to delay and expose visitors to corruption), the Watcher (a creature covered in dozens of eyes that steals the sight of its victims), and the Wounded Bushi (an amalgam of body parts from multiple warriors who seeks to sever and steal limbs from opponents).

### Candle Temple

A flame of hope flickers within Daylight Castle. Candle Temple, in the main keep, houses a shrine to the Fortunes — particularly Osano-wo, Fortune of Fire and Thunder. Corruption has utterly failed to penetrate this holy place, and it remains a sanctuary, inviolate and secure against the effects of the Shadowlands.
